    "summary": "Psychedelic Other One jam with stellar Brent organ work",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a soaring \"The Other One\" and tight Space transition, highlighted by Brent's superb organ on Little Red Rooster. A high‑energy Knockin' On Heaven's Door closes the night. Louie Louie kicks off an exciting second set.",
    "review": "On May 1, 1988 at Frost Amphitheatre, the Dead turned in a lively performance that captures the band’s late‑80s resurgence. The first set rolls through classics like \"Box of Rain\" and \"Hell In A Bucket,\" but it’s the second set that truly shines: a raucous \"Louie Louie\" launches into a seamless \"Truckin' → Crazy Fingers → Samson & Delilah → Eyes Of The World\" chain, leading to a propulsive Drums‑to‑Space segue. From there, Jerry ignites on the psychedelic \"The Other One,\" while Brent Mydland’s organ swells magnificently on Little Red Rooster and throughout the jam sections. Bill Kreutzmann drives the intensity into Space, and the band rides that momentum straight into a soaring encore of \"Knockin' On Heaven's Door.\" The set is rounded out by Bob Weir’s spirited vocals on \"Throwing Stones\" and solid, if occasionally understated, support from Phil Lesh.\n\nThe best listening experience comes from the soundboard capture (gd88-05-01.sbd.jerugim.2206.sbefail.shnf), which offers clear, balanced audio with a touch of quirky spatial character that adds to the live feel. Audience recordings are also good but lack the detail of the SBD. Overall, this show stands out for its energetic jams and Brent’s organ brilliance, making it a must‑listen for fans of the band’s late‑80s era.",
